TOPOLOGY AS TESTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The NTP client is the testbox itself. One cable carries both address families.

    tb277 eth3   fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1/64   (IPv6 NTP client + capture)
                 10.38.22.33/27              (IPv4 NTP client, comparison legs)
         |      ^
   NTP   |      |  NTP reply (mode 4, stratum 7)
 request |      |
         v      |
  +---------------------------------------------------------------------+
  |  swi_d   AT-x960-56YSQ         *** DUT -- the NTP server ***         |
  |  port1.0.10, access, vlan10                                          |
  |    IPv6 fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34/64   <- added by this case's setup   |
  |    IPv4 10.38.22.34/27                                               |
  +---------------------------------------------------------------------+

  DUT      : swi_d (AT-x960-56YSQ) console u2 @115200, S/N A2WQS700B,
             software sin_x96056-20261019-27. Not a stack: one unit.
  client   : tb277 itself. It is the NTP client for both address families and also the
             capture point.
  link     : tb277 eth3 -> swi_d port1.0.10 (copper, access port in vlan10). One cable.
             IPv6: eth3 fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1/64 <-> swi_d vlan10
             fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34/64. Both ends sit on one /64 with no router between
             them. The prefix is not invented: the testbox's eth3 already held
             fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1/64, and eth3 is cabled to port1.0.10.
             IPv4: eth3 10.38.22.33/27 <-> swi_d vlan10 10.38.22.34/27. Used in steps 3
             and 4 as the second address family against the same server.
  tools    : tools/ntpc.py, a crafted NTP client over a plain UDP socket, run on tb277,
             with `-6` for the IPv6 legs. It builds each 48-byte request itself, decodes
             every field of the reply, and reports origin_echo=yes when the reply echoes
             that request's transmit timestamp. Exit code 2 means no reply, which is a
             valid control result.
             tools/ntpwire.py, run on tb277 over each tcpdump capture. It counts NTP
             packets by address family, by mode and by address pair, and pairs each reply
             with its request.
  clocks   : swi_d and tb277 both run on UTC. Every time in this log is UTC. swi_d read
             `Wed, 21 Oct 2026 04:25:15 +0000` before step 1, aligned with the box.

