         reply stratum(s)=7 mode(s)=4
         offset min=-0.179137 max=-0.178943 mean=-0.179010 s

  ntpwire:
  11 NTP packets; by family: IPv6=11 ; by mode: client(3)=6 server(4)=5
  pairing: requests=6 replies=5 paired=5 unpaired=0 unanswered=1

  The one unanswered request out of six is the DEFAULT NTP rate limiter on this platform
  (burst 8, interval 8 s, leak 4, on by default and invisible in running-config). It is
  not a persistence failure: the five requests that were answered all carry stratum 7
  with the origin timestamp echoed.
  => STEP 5 PASS: BOTH halves of the saved configuration came back by themselves. The
     global IPv6 address on vlan10 came back, otherwise there would have been nothing to
     answer, and `ntp master 7` came back, because the replies carry stratum 7. The DUT
     was serving IPv6 time 11 s after the login prompt, with nothing typed into the
     device. The unit cold-booted to its login prompt in 61 s, and its clock drifted
     179 ms across the reboot, which is normal for a working real-time clock.

STEP 6 -- removal: the IPv6 server stops, and the counters go dark                 PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus(config)#no ntp master
awplus#show running-config | include ntp
  (nothing)
awplus#show ntp status
  (completely empty)

tb277$ tools/tb tool tb277 ntp1021 ntpc fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34 -6 \
         --bind fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1 --count 3 --gap 1 --timeout 3
SUMMARY: sent=3 replies=0 origin_echo=0 no_reply=3          (exit 2)
  => STEP 6 PASS: after `no ntp master` the DUT answers no IPv6 request. The IPv6 address
     was still on vlan10 and the leg was still pinging at this point, so the silence is
     the server being gone and not the leg being down. `show ntp status` is completely
     empty again, which matches the configuration exactly.
